{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ujf5nVFHEJta95PzQUmXDW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n4\t//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n5\t//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n6\t//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n7\t//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n8\t//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n9\t//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n10\t//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n11\t//! companions.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Phase 4: all function bodies are implemented.  The empty test\n14\t//! module will be expanded in later phases.\n15\t//!\n16\t//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n17\t//!\n18\t//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n19\t//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n20\t//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n21\t//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n22\t//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n23\t//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n24\t//!   writer alive between calls.\n25\t//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` collapse to direct `std::fs::File`\n26\t//!   use at call sites; [`file_delete`] keeps the portable delete\n27\t//!   helper as an OS-backed function.\n28\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n29\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n30\t//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n31\t//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n32\t//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n33\t//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n34\t//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n35\t//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n36\t//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n37\t//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n38\t//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n39\t//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n40\t//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n41\t//! * Threading wrappers from the C portability layer map directly to\n42\t//!   Rust standard-library types at call sites: `std::thread`,\n43\t//!   `std::sync::Mutex`, channels, and condition variables.\n44\t//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n45\t//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n46\t//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n47\t//!   are call-site macros rather than standalone functions; translated\n48\t//!   call sites record equivalent wake provenance when their owning\n49\t//!   connection or QUIC context exposes it.\n50\t\n51\tuse core::cmp::Ordering;\n52\tuse core::net::{IpAddr, SocketAddr};\n53\tuse std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\n54\tuse std::sync::Mutex;\n55\t\n56\tuse crate::Error;\n57\tuse crate::{CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE, ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n58\t\n59\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n60\t// Tracing / file-id constants.\n61\t//\n62\t// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n63\t// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n64\t// itself is translated at the C macro's call sites.\n65\t\n66\t/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\n67\tpub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n68\t/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\n69\tpub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n70\t/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\n71\tpub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n72\t/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\n73\tpub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n74\t/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\n75\tpub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n76\t\n77\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n78\t// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n79\t\n80\t/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n81\t/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\n82\tpub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n83\t    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n84\t}\n85\t\n86\t/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n87\t/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\n88\tpub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n89\t    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n93\t// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n94\t// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n95\t// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n96\t\n97\t/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\n98\tpub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n99\t/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\n100\tpub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n101\t\n102\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n103\t// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n104\t\n105\t/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n106\t/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n107\t/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\n108\tpub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n109\t\n110\t// Thread-local debug state — no Send required; library is single-threaded.\n111\tthread_local! {\n112\t    static DEBUG_OUT: RefCell<Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>> = const { RefCell::new(None) };\n113\t    static DEBUG_SUSPENDED: Cell<bool> = const { Cell::new(false) };\n114\t}\n115\t\n116\t// Global solution_dir; typically set once at startup.  Box::leak gives the\n117\t// returned reference a 'static lifetime at the cost of a one-time allocation.\n118\tstatic SOLUTION_DIR: Mutex<Option<&'static str>> = Mutex::new(None);\n119\t\n120\t/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n121\t/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n122\t/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n123\t/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n124\t/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n125\t/// alternative.\n126\tpub fn debug_set_stream(stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n127\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| *o.borrow_mut() = stream);\n128\t}\n129\t\n130\t/// Returns `true` if a debug output sink is currently installed.\n131\t/// C: `picoquic/util.c:get_debug_out` — callers that checked\n132\t/// `get_debug_out() != NULL` before calling `fprintf` map to\n133\t/// checking this boolean.\n134\tpub fn get_debug_out() -> bool {\n135\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| o.borrow().is_some())\n136\t}\n137\t\n138\t/// Returns `true` if debug output is currently suspended.\n139\t/// C: `picoquic/util.c:get_debug_suspended` — the C `int` (0/1)\n140\t/// maps to `bool`.\n141\tpub fn get_debug_suspended() -> bool {\n142\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get())\n143\t}\n144\t\n145\t/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n146\t/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n147\t/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n148\t/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n149\t/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\n150\tpub fn debug_printf(msg: &str) {\n151\t    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n152\t        return;\n153\t    }\n154\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n155\t        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n156\t            let _ = w.write_str(msg);\n157\t        }\n158\t    });\n159\t}\n160\t\n161\t/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n162\t/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n163\t/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n164\t/// to restore.\n165\tpub fn debug_printf_push_stream(stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n166\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n167\t        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n168\t        assert!(\n169\t            guard.is_none(),\n170\t            \"nested debug_printf_push_stream not supported\"\n171\t        );\n172\t        *guard = Some(stream);\n173\t    });\n174\t}\n175\t\n176\t/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n177\t/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\n178\tpub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n179\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n180\t        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n181\t        assert!(\n182\t            guard.is_some(),\n183\t            \"debug_printf_pop_stream: no current stream\"\n184\t        );\n185\t        *guard = None;\n186\t    });\n187\t}\n188\t\n189\t/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n190\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\n191\tpub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n192\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(true));\n193\t}\n194\t\n195\t/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n196\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\n197\tpub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n198\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(false));\n199\t}\n200\t\n201\t/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n202\t/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n203\t/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n204\t/// `bool`.\n205\tpub fn debug_printf_reset(suspended: bool) -> bool {\n206\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| {\n207\t        let old = s.get();\n208\t        s.set(suspended);\n209\t        old\n210\t    })\n211\t}\n212\t\n213\t/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n214\t/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n215\t/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n216\t/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\n217\tpub fn debug_dump(bytes: &[u8]) {\n218\t    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n219\t        return;\n220\t    }\n221\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n222\t        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n223\t            for (i, chunk) in bytes.chunks(16).enumerate() {\n224\t                let _ = write!(w, \"{:04x}:  \", i * 16);\n225\t                for b in chunk {\n226\t                    let _ = write!(w, \"{:02x} \", b);\n227\t                }\n228\t                let _ = writeln!(w);\n229\t            }\n230\t        }\n231\t    });\n232\t}\n233\t\n234\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n235\t// String utilities.\n236\t\n237\t// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n238\t// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n239\t// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n240\t// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n241\t// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n242\t\n243\t/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n244\t/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n245\t/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n246\t/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n247\t///\n248\t/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n249\t/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n250\t/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n251\t/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n252\t/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\n253\tpub fn sprintf(buf: &mut [u8], msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n254\t    let bytes = msg.as_bytes();\n255\t    if bytes.len() < buf.len() {\n256\t        buf[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n257\t        buf[bytes.len()] = 0;\n258\t        Ok(bytes.len())\n259\t    } else if !buf.is_empty() {\n260\t        let n = buf.len() - 1;\n261\t        buf[..n].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..n]);\n262\t        buf[n] = 0;\n263\t        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n264\t    } else {\n265\t        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n266\t    }\n267\t}\n268\t\n269\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n270\t// Connection-id helpers.\n271\t\n272\t/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n273\t/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\n274\tpub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n275\t    id: [0; 20],\n276\t    id_len: 0,\n277\t};\n278\t\n279\t/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n280\t/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n281\t/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n282\t///\n283\t/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n284\t/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n285\t/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n286\t/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\n287\tpub fn format_connection_id(bytes: &mut [u8], cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n288\t    let id_bytes = cnx_id.as_bytes();\n289\t    let copied = id_bytes.len();\n290\t    if copied == 0 || copied > bytes.len() {\n291\t        return 0;\n292\t    }\n293\t    bytes[..copied].copy_from_slice(id_bytes);\n294\t    copied as u8\n295\t}\n296\t\n297\t/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n298\t/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n299\t/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n300\t/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n301\t///\n302\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n303\t/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n304\t/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n305\t/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\n306\tpub fn parse_connection_id(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n307\t    let len = bytes.len();\n308\t    if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n309\t        return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n310\t    }\n311\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n312\t    id[..len].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n313\t    Ok(ConnectionId {\n314\t        id,\n315\t        id_len: len as u8,\n316\t    })\n317\t}\n318\t\n319\t/// Three-way compare two connection ids.  Shorter length sorts\n320\t/// before longer; equal-length ids are compared byte-by-byte.\n321\t/// C: `picoquic/util.c:picoquic_compare_connection_id`\n322\tpub fn compare_connection_id(id1: &ConnectionId, id2: &ConnectionId) -> Ordering {\n323\t    let len1 = id1.as_bytes().len();\n324\t    let len2 = id2.as_bytes().len();\n325\t    match len1.cmp(&len2) {\n326\t        Ordering::Equal => id1.as_bytes().cmp(id2.as_bytes()),\n327\t        other => other,\n328\t    }\n329\t}\n330\t\n331\t// `is_null` / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n332\t// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n333\t// C source.  The `Ord` impl on `ConnectionId` and the free\n334\t// `compare_connection_id` above match the C three-way comparator.\n335\t\n336\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n337\t// Address helpers.\n338\t\n339\t/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n340\t/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n341\t/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n342\t/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n343\t/// address + 2 for the port).\n344\tpub fn hash_addr_bytes(addr: &SocketAddr, bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n345\t    let mut l = 0;\n346\t    match addr {\n347\t        SocketAddr::V4(a) => {\n348\t            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n349\t            bytes[l..l + 4].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n350\t            l += 4;\n351\t            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n352\t            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n353\t            l += 2;\n354\t        }\n355\t        SocketAddr::V6(a) => {\n356\t            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n357\t            bytes[l..l + 16].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n358\t            l += 16;\n359\t            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n360\t            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n361\t            l += 2;\n362\t        }\n363\t    }\n364\t    l\n365\t}\n366\t\n367\t/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n368\t/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n369\t/// hash_seed)`.\n370\tpub fn hash_addr(addr: &SocketAddr, hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n371\t    let mut bytes = [0u8; 18];\n372\t    let l = hash_addr_bytes(addr, &mut bytes);\n373\t    crate::siphash::siphash(&bytes[..l], hash_seed)\n374\t}\n375\t\n376\t/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n377\t/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n378\t/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n379\t/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n380\t///\n381\t/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\n382\tpub fn parse_hexa(hex_input: &str, bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n383\t    fn hexa_digit(x: u8) -> Option<u8> {\n384\t        match x {\n385\t            b'0'..=b'9' => Some(x - b'0'),\n386\t            b'A'..=b'F' => Some(x - b'A' + 10),\n387\t            b'a'..=b'f' => Some(x - b'a' + 10),\n388\t            _ => None,\n389\t        }\n390\t    }\n391\t    let inp = hex_input.as_bytes();\n392\t    let input_length = inp.len();\n393\t    if input_length == 0 || (input_length & 1) != 0 || 2 * bin_output.len() < input_length {\n394\t        return 0;\n395\t    }\n396\t    let mut ret = 0;\n397\t    let mut offset = 0;\n398\t    while offset < input_length {\n399\t        match (hexa_digit(inp[offset]), hexa_digit(inp[offset + 1])) {\n400\t            (Some(av), Some(bv)) => {\n401\t                bin_output[ret] = (av << 4) | bv;\n402\t                ret += 1;\n403\t            }\n404\t            _ => return 0,\n405\t        }\n406\t        offset += 2;\n407\t    }\n408\t    ret\n409\t}\n410\t\n411\t/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n412\t/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n413\t/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n414\t/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n415\t/// `Result` return.\n416\tpub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n417\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n418\t    let len = parse_hexa(hex_input, &mut id[..18]);\n419\t    Ok(ConnectionId {\n420\t        id,\n421\t        id_len: len as u8,\n422\t    })\n423\t}\n424\t\n425\t/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n426\t/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n427\t/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n428\t///\n429\t/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n430\t/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n431\t/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\n432\tpub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n433\t    w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n434\t    connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n435\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n436\t    for b in connection_id.as_bytes() {\n437\t        write!(w, \"{:02x}\", b).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)?;\n438\t    }\n439\t    Ok(())\n440\t}\n441\t\n442\t/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n443\t/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n444\t/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\n445\tpub fn compare_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n446\t    let ip_cmp = compare_ip_addr(expected, actual);\n447\t    if ip_cmp == Ordering::Equal {\n448\t        expected.port().cmp(&actual.port())\n449\t    } else {\n450\t        ip_cmp\n451\t    }\n452\t}\n453\t\n454\t/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n455\t/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n456\t/// struct sockaddr*)`.\n457\tpub fn compare_ip_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n458\t    match (expected, actual) {\n459\t        (SocketAddr::V4(ex), SocketAddr::V4(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n460\t        (SocketAddr::V6(ex), SocketAddr::V6(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n461\t        // AF_INET (2) < AF_INET6 (10 on Linux), so V4 < V6.\n462\t        (SocketAddr::V4(_), SocketAddr::V6(_)) => Ordering::Less,\n463\t        (SocketAddr::V6(_), SocketAddr::V4(_)) => Ordering::Greater,\n464\t    }\n465\t}\n466\t\n467\t/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n468\t/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n469\tpub fn get_addr_port(addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n470\t    addr.port()\n471\t}\n472\t\n473\t/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n474\t/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n475\t/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n476\t/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\n477\tpub fn set_addr_port(addr: &mut SocketAddr, port: u16) {\n478\t    addr.set_port(port);\n479\t}\n480\t\n481\t/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n482\t/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n483\t/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n484\t/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n485\t/// `usize`.\n486\tpub fn addr_length(addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n487\t    match addr {\n488\t        SocketAddr::V4(_) => 16, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)\n489\t        SocketAddr::V6(_) => 28, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in6)\n490\t    }\n491\t}\n492\t\n493\t/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n494\t/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n495\t/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n496\t/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n497\t///\n498\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n499\t/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\n500\tpub fn store_addr(addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n501\t    addr.copied()\n502\t}\n503\t\n504\t/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n505\t/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n506\t/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n507\t/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n508\t/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\n509\tpub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n510\t    // `SocketAddr` exposes IP octets only by value (`IpAddr::octets()`),\n511\t    // not by reference; safe Rust cannot produce the required borrow.\n512\t    // Callers should use `addr.ip()` with `Ipv4Addr::octets()` /\n513\t    // `Ipv6Addr::octets()` directly.\n514\t    None\n515\t}\n516\t\n517\t/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n518\t/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n519\t/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n520\t/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\n521\tpub fn store_text_addr(ip_address_text: &str, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n522\t    let ip: IpAddr = ip_address_text\n523\t        .parse()\n524\t        .map_err(|_| Error::InvalidArgument)?;\n525\t    Ok(SocketAddr::new(ip, port))\n526\t}\n527\t\n528\t/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n529\t/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n530\t/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n531\t/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n532\t/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\n533\tpub fn addr_text(addr: &SocketAddr, w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n534\t    match addr {\n535\t        SocketAddr::V4(a) => write!(w, \"{}:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n536\t        SocketAddr::V6(a) => write!(w, \"[{}]:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n537\t    }\n538\t}\n539\t\n540\t/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n541\t/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n542\t/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n543\t/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n544\t/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n545\t///\n546\t/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n547\t/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\n548\tpub fn store_loopback_addr(addr_family: i32, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n549\t    // AF_INET=2 is universal; AF_INET6=10 on Linux, 30 on macOS.\n550\t    const AF_INET: i32 = 2;\n551\t    #[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\n552\t    const AF_INET6: i32 = 30;\n553\t    #[cfg(not(target_os = \"macos\"))]\n554\t    const AF_INET6: i32 = 10;\n555\t\n556\t    if addr_family == AF_INET {\n557\t        store_text_addr(\"127.0.0.1\", port)\n558\t    } else if addr_family == AF_INET6 {\n559\t        store_text_addr(\"::1\", port)\n560\t    } else {\n561\t        Err(Error::InvalidArgument)\n562\t    }\n563\t}\n564\t\n565\t/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n566\t/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n567\t/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n568\t/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n569\t///\n570\t/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n571\t/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n572\t/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n573\t/// the C behaviour.\n574\tpub fn set_preferred_address(\n575\t    preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n576\t    v4_text: Option<&str>,\n577\t    v6_text: Option<&str>,\n578\t    preferred_port: u16,\n579\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n580\t    *preferred = PreferredAddress::default();\n581\t    if let Some(v4) = v4_text {\n582\t        let addr = store_text_addr(v4, preferred_port)?;\n583\t        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V4(_)) {\n584\t            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n585\t        }\n586\t        preferred.v4 = Some(addr);\n587\t    }\n588\t    if let Some(v6) = v6_text {\n589\t        let addr = store_text_addr(v6, preferred_port)?;\n590\t        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V6(_)) {\n591\t            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n592\t        }\n593\t        preferred.v6 = Some(addr);\n594\t    }\n595\t    Ok(())\n596\t}\n597\t\n598\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n599\t// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n600\t\n601\t/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n602\t/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n603\t/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\n604\tpub fn set_solution_dir(solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n605\t    let mut lock = SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap();\n606\t    *lock = solution_dir.map(|s| -> &'static str { Box::leak(s.to_owned().into_boxed_str()) });\n607\t}\n608\t\n609\t/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n610\t/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n611\t/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\n612\tpub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n613\t    *SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap()\n614\t}\n615\t\n616\t/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n617\t/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n618\t/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n619\t/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n620\t/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\n621\tpub fn get_input_path(\n622\t    target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n623\t    solution_path: Option<&str>,\n624\t    file_name: &str,\n625\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n626\t    let solution_path = solution_path.unwrap_or(DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR);\n627\t    let sep = if solution_path.ends_with(FILE_SEPARATOR) {\n628\t        \"\"\n629\t    } else {\n630\t        FILE_SEPARATOR\n631\t    };\n632\t    write!(target_file_path, \"{}{}{}\", solution_path, sep, file_name).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)\n633\t}\n634\t\n635\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n636\t// Portable file open / close.\n637\t//\n638\t// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n639\t// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n640\t// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n641\t//\n642\t// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n643\t// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n644\t// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n645\t// feature.\n646\t\n647\t/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n648\t/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\n649\tpub fn file_delete(file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n650\t    std::fs::remove_file(file_name).map_err(|e| e.raw_os_error().unwrap_or(-1))\n651\t}\n652\t\n653\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n654\t// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n655\t//\n656\t// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n657\t// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n658\t// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n659\t// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n660\t// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n661\t// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n662\t// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n663\t// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n664\t\n665\t/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n666\t/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\n667\tpub fn frames_fixed_skip(bytes: &[u8], size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n668\t    let size = usize::try_from(size).ok()?;\n669\t    bytes.get(size..)\n670\t}\n671\t\n672\t/// Skip a varint-encoded field; returns the suffix.  C:\n673\t/// `frames_varint_skip`.\n674\tpub fn frames_varint_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n675\t    let v_len = varint_len(*bytes.first()?);\n676\t    frames_fixed_skip(bytes, v_len as u64)\n677\t}\n678\t\n679\t/// Decode a varint and return the suffix plus the value.  C:\n680\t/// `frames_varint_decode` with the `*n64` out-parameter\n681\t/// folded into the tuple return.\n682\tpub fn frames_varint_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n683\t    let first = *bytes.first()?;\n684\t    let length = varint_len(first);\n685\t    if bytes.len() < length {\n686\t        return None;\n687\t    }\n688\t    let mut v = (first & 0x3F) as u64;\n689\t    for &b in &bytes[1..length] {\n690\t        v <<= 8;\n691\t        v += b as u64;\n692\t    }\n693\t    Some((&bytes[length..], v))\n694\t}\n695\t\n696\t/// Decode a length-prefixed field's length component as a\n697\t/// `usize`.  C: `frames_varlen_decode`.\n698\tpub fn frames_varlen_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], usize)> {\n699\t    let (rest, len) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n700\t    let n = usize::try_from(len).ok()?;\n701\t    Some((rest, n))\n702\t}\n703\t\n704\t/// Decode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_decode`.\n705\tpub fn frames_uint8_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u8)> {\n706\t    let (&n, rest) = bytes.split_first()?;\n707\t    Some((rest, n))\n708\t}\n709\t\n710\t/// Decode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_decode`.\n711\tpub fn frames_uint16_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u16)> {\n712\t    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n713\t        return None;\n714\t    }\n715\t    let n = u16::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1]]);\n716\t    Some((&bytes[2..], n))\n717\t}\n718\t\n719\t/// Decode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_decode`.\n720\tpub fn frames_uint32_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u32)> {\n721\t    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n722\t        return None;\n723\t    }\n724\t    let n = u32::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]]);\n725\t    Some((&bytes[4..], n))\n726\t}\n727\t\n728\t/// Decode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_decode`.\n729\tpub fn frames_uint64_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n730\t    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n731\t        return None;\n732\t    }\n733\t    let n = u64::from_be_bytes(bytes[..8].try_into().unwrap());\n734\t    Some((&bytes[8..], n))\n735\t}\n736\t\n737\t/// Skip a length-prefixed data field.  C:\n738\t/// `frames_length_data_skip`.\n739\tpub fn frames_length_data_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n740\t    let (rest, length) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n741\t    frames_fixed_skip(rest, length)\n742\t}\n743\t\n744\t/// Decode a connection id and return the suffix.  C:\n745\t/// `frames_cid_decode`.  The output parameter folds into\n746\t/// the tuple return.\n747\tpub fn frames_cid_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], ConnectionId)> {\n748\t    let (rest, id_len) = frames_uint8_decode(bytes)?;\n749\t    if id_len as usize > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE || rest.len() < id_len as usize {\n750\t        return None;\n751\t    }\n752\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n753\t    id[..id_len as usize].copy_from_slice(&rest[..id_len as usize]);\n754\t    Some((&rest[id_len as usize..], ConnectionId { id, id_len }))\n755\t}\n756\t\n757\t/// Length of the varint encoding of `n64`.  C:\n758\t/// `size_t frames_varint_encode_length(uint64_t n64)`.\n759\tpub fn frames_varint_encode_length(n64: u64) -> usize {\n760\t    if n64 < 64 {\n761\t        1\n762\t    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n763\t        2\n764\t    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n765\t        4\n766\t    } else {\n767\t        8\n768\t    }\n769\t}\n770\t\n771\t/// Decode the wire length of a varint from its first byte.  C\n772\t/// macro: `VARINT_LEN(bytes) (((uint8_t)1) << ((bytes[0] >> 6) & 3))`.\n773\t/// The macro takes the whole buffer; the function takes just the\n774\t/// first byte (the rest is unused in the C expansion).\n775\tpub const fn varint_len(byte0: u8) -> usize {\n776\t    1usize << ((byte0 >> 6) & 3)\n777\t}\n778\t\n779\t/// Encode a varint into `bytes`; returns the unused tail or `None`\n780\t/// if the buffer was too small.  C: `frames_varint_encode`.\n781\tpub fn frames_varint_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n782\t    if n64 < 64 {\n783\t        if bytes.is_empty() {\n784\t            return None;\n785\t        }\n786\t        bytes[0] = n64 as u8;\n787\t        Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n788\t    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n789\t        if bytes.len() < 2 {\n790\t            return None;\n791\t        }\n792\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 8) | 0x40) as u8;\n793\t        bytes[1] = n64 as u8;\n794\t        Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n795\t    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n796\t        if bytes.len() < 4 {\n797\t            return None;\n798\t        }\n799\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 24) | 0x80) as u8;\n800\t        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n801\t        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n802\t        bytes[3] = n64 as u8;\n803\t        Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n804\t    } else {\n805\t        if bytes.len() < 8 {\n806\t            return None;\n807\t        }\n808\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 56) | 0xC0) as u8;\n809\t        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n810\t        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n811\t        bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n812\t        bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n813\t        bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n814\t        bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n815\t        bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n816\t        Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n817\t    }\n818\t}\n819\t\n820\t/// Encode a `usize` as a varint length prefix.  C:\n821\t/// `frames_varlen_encode`.\n822\tpub fn frames_varlen_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: usize) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n823\t    frames_varint_encode(bytes, n as u64)\n824\t}\n825\t\n826\t/// Encode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_encode`.\n827\tpub fn frames_uint8_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u8) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n828\t    if bytes.is_empty() {\n829\t        return None;\n830\t    }\n831\t    bytes[0] = n;\n832\t    Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n833\t}\n834\t\n835\t/// Encode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_encode`.\n836\tpub fn frames_uint16_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u16) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n837\t    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n838\t        return None;\n839\t    }\n840\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n841\t    bytes[1] = n as u8;\n842\t    Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n843\t}\n844\t\n845\t/// Encode a 24-bit value (low three bytes of `n`) in network\n846\t/// order.  C: `frames_uint24_encode`.\n847\tpub fn frames_uint24_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n848\t    if bytes.len() < 3 {\n849\t        return None;\n850\t    }\n851\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n852\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n853\t    bytes[2] = n as u8;\n854\t    Some(&mut bytes[3..])\n855\t}\n856\t\n857\t/// Encode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_encode`.\n858\tpub fn frames_uint32_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n859\t    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n860\t        return None;\n861\t    }\n862\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n863\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n864\t    bytes[2] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n865\t    bytes[3] = n as u8;\n866\t    Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n867\t}\n868\t\n869\t/// Encode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_encode`.\n870\tpub fn frames_uint64_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n871\t    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n872\t        return None;\n873\t    }\n874\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 56) as u8;\n875\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 48) as u8;\n876\t    bytes[2] = (n >> 40) as u8;\n877\t    bytes[3] = (n >> 32) as u8;\n878\t    bytes[4] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n879\t    bytes[5] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n880\t    bytes[6] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n881\t    bytes[7] = n as u8;\n882\t    Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n883\t}\n884\t\n885\t/// Encode a length prefix followed by the data bytes.  C:\n886\t/// `frames_length_data_encode`.  The C signature took\n887\t/// `(size_t l, const uint8_t* v)` — collapsed to `&[u8]` (slice\n888\t/// length subsumes `l`).\n889\tpub fn frames_length_data_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], v: &[u8]) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n890\t    let l = v.len();\n891\t    let rest = frames_varlen_encode(bytes, l)?;\n892\t    if rest.len() < l {\n893\t        return None;\n894\t    }\n895\t    rest[..l].copy_from_slice(v);\n896\t    Some(&mut rest[l..])\n897\t}\n898\t\n899\t/// Encode a connection id as length-prefixed data.  C:\n900\t/// `frames_cid_encode`.\n901\tpub fn frames_cid_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], cid: &ConnectionId) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n902\t    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, cid.as_bytes())\n903\t}\n904\t\n905\t/// Encode a NUL-terminated C string `s` as length-prefixed data\n906\t/// (the NUL is *not* written).  C: `frames_charz_encode`.\n907\tpub fn frames_charz_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], s: &str) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n908\t    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, s.as_bytes())\n909\t}\n910\t\n911\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n912\t// Fixed-width big-endian frame-field writers (intformat.c).\n913\t//\n914\t// Write exactly N bytes of a big-endian integer into the start of\n915\t// `bytes`, panicking if the slice is too short (same as the C\n916\t// contract: passing an undersized buffer is undefined behaviour).\n917\t\n918\t/// Write `n16` as a big-endian 16-bit value into `bytes[0..2]`.\n919\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:27-31`.\n920\tpub fn picoformat_16(bytes: &mut [u8], n16: u16) {\n921\t    bytes[0] = (n16 >> 8) as u8;\n922\t    bytes[1] = n16 as u8;\n923\t}\n924\t\n925\t/// Write the low 24 bits of `n24` as a big-endian 3-byte value into `bytes[0..3]`.\n926\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:33-38`.\n927\tpub fn picoformat_24(bytes: &mut [u8], n24: u32) {\n928\t    bytes[0] = (n24 >> 16) as u8;\n929\t    bytes[1] = (n24 >> 8) as u8;\n930\t    bytes[2] = n24 as u8;\n931\t}\n932\t\n933\t/// Write `n32` as a big-endian 32-bit value into `bytes[0..4]`.\n934\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:40-46`.\n935\tpub fn picoformat_32(bytes: &mut [u8], n32: u32) {\n936\t    bytes[0] = (n32 >> 24) as u8;\n937\t    bytes[1] = (n32 >> 16) as u8;\n938\t    bytes[2] = (n32 >> 8) as u8;\n939\t    bytes[3] = n32 as u8;\n940\t}\n941\t\n942\t/// Write `n64` as a big-endian 64-bit value into `bytes[0..8]`.\n943\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:48-58`.\n944\tpub fn picoformat_64(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) {\n945\t    bytes[0] = (n64 >> 56) as u8;\n946\t    bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n947\t    bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n948\t    bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n949\t    bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n950\t    bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n951\t    bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n952\t    bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n953\t}\n954\t\n955\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n956\t// Constant-time memcmp (used for reset secrets).\n957\t\n958\t/// Constant-time three-way compare of two byte buffers of equal\n959\t/// length.  C: `int constant_time_memcmp(const uint8_t*\n960\t/// x, const uint8_t* y, size_t l)` — returning negative / zero /\n961\t/// positive, mapped to [`Ordering`].  The two slice lengths are\n962\t/// the C `l` argument and must match.\n963\tpub fn constant_time_memcmp(x: &[u8], y: &[u8]) -> Ordering {\n964\t    let mut acc: u64 = 0;\n965\t    for (&xi, &yi) in x.iter().zip(y.iter()) {\n966\t        acc += (xi ^ yi) as u64;\n967\t    }\n968\t    if acc == 0 {\n969\t        Ordering::Equal\n970\t    } else {\n971\t        Ordering::Less\n972\t    }\n973\t}\n974\t\n975\t// The C `picoquic_thread_t` / `picoquic_mutex_t` / `picoquic_event_t`\n976\t// portability wrappers around pthread / Win32 are gone.  Rust call\n977\t// sites use the standard library directly: `std::thread::JoinHandle`\n978\t// for a thread handle, `std::sync::Mutex<T>` for a lock paired with\n979\t// the value it guards, and `(Mutex<bool>, Condvar)` (or a higher-level\n980\t// channel from `std::sync::mpsc`) where C used `picoquic_event_t`.\n981\t//\n982\t// Likewise the `picoquic_thread_fn` typedef has no Rust counterpart —\n983\t// `std::thread::spawn` already takes an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send`\n984\t// closure, which subsumes the C \"function pointer + void* arg\"\n985\t// pattern.\n986\t\n987\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n988\t// Random-number helpers.\n989\t\n990\t// `uniform_random` is gone -- the C body read `/dev/urandom`\n991\t// at every call.  Rust callers use `rand::Rng::random_range`\n992\t// on whatever RNG they hold (`Quic.rng` for the secure stream,\n993\t// `rand::rng()` for the non-secure thread-local).\n994\t\n995\t// `test_random` and the `test_*_random` family moved to\n996\t// `crate::tests::util` — they only support the test suite.\n997\t\n998\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n999\t// Byte-array → text conversion (logging helper).\n1000\t\n1001\t/// Translate a byte buffer to a printable text form.  C:\n1002\t/// `char* uint8_to_str(char* text, size_t text_len, const\n1003\t/// uint8_t* data, size_t data_len)` — both length parameters fold\n1004\t/// into the slice lengths.  The returned slice is the populated\n1005\t/// prefix of `text`.\n1006\tpub fn uint8_to_str<'a>(text: &'a mut [u8], data: &[u8]) -> &'a [u8] {\n1007\t    let text_len = text.len();\n1008\t    let data_len = data.len();\n1009\t    let render_length = if data_len >= text_len {\n1010\t        text_len.saturating_sub(4)\n1011\t    } else {\n1012\t        data_len\n1013\t    };\n1014\t    let mut rendered = 0usize;\n1015\t    for &c in &data[..render_length] {\n1016\t        text[rendered] = if (b' '..127).contains(&c) { c } else { b'?' };\n1017\t        rendered += 1;\n1018\t    }\n1019\t    if rendered < data_len {\n1020\t        let mut i = 0usize;\n1021\t        while i < 3 && rendered + 1 < text_len {\n1022\t            text[rendered] = b'.';\n1023\t            rendered += 1;\n1024\t            i += 1;\n1025\t        }\n1026\t    }\n1027\t    if rendered < text_len {\n1028\t        text[rendered] = 0;\n1029\t    }\n1030\t    &text[..rendered]\n1031\t}\n1032\t\n1033\t// `TestSimPacket`, `TestAqm`, `JitterMode`, and `TestSimLink`\n1034\t// moved to `crate::tests::util` — they translate the\n1035\t// `picoquictest/sim_link.c` test-only network simulator.\n1036\t// `TEST_SNI` and the `TEST_FILE_*` / `TEST_ECH_*` cert-path\n1037\t// constants likewise moved over.\n1038\t\n1039\t#[cfg(test)]\n1040\tmod test {}\n1041\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da743372-a94e-4c3a-8252-a357471bf140","uuid":"e64ebc79-6ca9-4e76-995a-45dd0bb987df","timestamp":"2026-05-07T10:12:29.910Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/utils.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n//!\n//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n//! companions.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: all function bodies are implemented.  The empty test\n//! module will be expanded in later phases.\n//!\n//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n//!\n//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n//!   writer alive between calls.\n//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` collapse to direct `std::fs::File`\n//!   use at call sites; [`file_delete`] keeps the portable delete\n//!   helper as an OS-backed function.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n//! * Threading wrappers from the C portability layer map directly to\n//!   Rust standard-library types at call sites: `std::thread`,\n//!   `std::sync::Mutex`, channels, and condition variables.\n//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n//!   are call-site macros rather than standalone functions; translated\n//!   call sites record equivalent wake provenance when their owning\n//!   connection or QUIC context exposes it.\n\nuse core::cmp::Ordering;\nuse core::net::{IpAddr, SocketAddr};\nuse std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::sync::Mutex;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::{CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE, ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Tracing / file-id constants.\n//\n// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n// itself is translated at the C macro's call sites.\n\n/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\npub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\npub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\npub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\npub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\npub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n\n/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\npub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n}\n\n/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\npub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n\n/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\npub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\npub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n\n/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\npub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n\n// Thread-local debug state — no Send required; library is single-threaded.\nthread_local! {\n    static DEBUG_OUT: RefCell<Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>> = const { RefCell::new(None) };\n    static DEBUG_SUSPENDED: Cell<bool> = const { Cell::new(false) };\n}\n\n// Global solution_dir; typically set once at startup.  Box::leak gives the\n// returned reference a 'static lifetime at the cost of a one-time allocation.\nstatic SOLUTION_DIR: Mutex<Option<&'static str>> = Mutex::new(None);\n\n/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n/// alternative.\npub fn debug_set_stream(stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| *o.borrow_mut() = stream);\n}\n\n/// Returns `true` if a debug output sink is currently installed.\n/// C: `picoquic/util.c:get_debug_out` — callers that checked\n/// `get_debug_out() != NULL` before calling `fprintf` map to\n/// checking this boolean.\npub fn get_debug_out() -> bool {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| o.borrow().is_some())\n}\n\n/// Returns `true` if debug output is currently suspended.\n/// C: `picoquic/util.c:get_debug_suspended` — the C `int` (0/1)\n/// maps to `bool`.\npub fn get_debug_suspended() -> bool {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get())\n}\n\n/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\npub fn debug_printf(msg: &str) {\n    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n        return;\n    }\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n            let _ = w.write_str(msg);\n        }\n    });\n}\n\n/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n/// to restore.\npub fn debug_printf_push_stream(stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n        assert!(\n            guard.is_none(),\n            \"nested debug_printf_push_stream not supported\"\n        );\n        *guard = Some(stream);\n    });\n}\n\n/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n        assert!(\n            guard.is_some(),\n            \"debug_printf_pop_stream: no current stream\"\n        );\n        *guard = None;\n    });\n}\n\n/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(true));\n}\n\n/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(false));\n}\n\n/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n/// `bool`.\npub fn debug_printf_reset(suspended: bool) -> bool {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| {\n        let old = s.get();\n        s.set(suspended);\n        old\n    })\n}\n\n/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\npub fn debug_dump(bytes: &[u8]) {\n    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n        return;\n    }\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n            for (i, chunk) in bytes.chunks(16).enumerate() {\n                let _ = write!(w, \"{:04x}:  \", i * 16);\n                for b in chunk {\n                    let _ = write!(w, \"{:02x} \", b);\n                }\n                let _ = writeln!(w);\n            }\n        }\n    });\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// String utilities.\n\n// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n\n/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n///\n/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\npub fn sprintf(buf: &mut [u8], msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n    let bytes = msg.as_bytes();\n    if bytes.len() < buf.len() {\n        buf[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n        buf[bytes.len()] = 0;\n        Ok(bytes.len())\n    } else if !buf.is_empty() {\n        let n = buf.len() - 1;\n        buf[..n].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..n]);\n        buf[n] = 0;\n        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n    } else {\n        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Connection-id helpers.\n\n/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\npub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n    id: [0; 20],\n    id_len: 0,\n};\n\n/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n///\n/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\npub fn format_connection_id(bytes: &mut [u8], cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n    let id_bytes = cnx_id.as_bytes();\n    let copied = id_bytes.len();\n    if copied == 0 || copied > bytes.len() {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    bytes[..copied].copy_from_slice(id_bytes);\n    copied as u8\n}\n\n/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\npub fn parse_connection_id(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    let len = bytes.len();\n    if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n        return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n    }\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    id[..len].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n    Ok(ConnectionId {\n        id,\n        id_len: len as u8,\n    })\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare two connection ids.  Shorter length sorts\n/// before longer; equal-length ids are compared byte-by-byte.\n/// C: `picoquic/util.c:picoquic_compare_connection_id`\npub fn compare_connection_id(id1: &ConnectionId, id2: &ConnectionId) -> Ordering {\n    let len1 = id1.as_bytes().len();\n    let len2 = id2.as_bytes().len();\n    match len1.cmp(&len2) {\n        Ordering::Equal => id1.as_bytes().cmp(id2.as_bytes()),\n        other => other,\n    }\n}\n\n// `is_null` / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n// C source.  The `Ord` impl on `ConnectionId` and the free\n// `compare_connection_id` above match the C three-way comparator.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Address helpers.\n\n/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n/// address + 2 for the port).\npub fn hash_addr_bytes(addr: &SocketAddr, bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    let mut l = 0;\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(a) => {\n            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n            bytes[l..l + 4].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n            l += 4;\n            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n            l += 2;\n        }\n        SocketAddr::V6(a) => {\n            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n            bytes[l..l + 16].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n            l += 16;\n            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n            l += 2;\n        }\n    }\n    l\n}\n\n/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n/// hash_seed)`.\npub fn hash_addr(addr: &SocketAddr, hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n    let mut bytes = [0u8; 18];\n    let l = hash_addr_bytes(addr, &mut bytes);\n    crate::siphash::siphash(&bytes[..l], hash_seed)\n}\n\n/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n///\n/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\npub fn parse_hexa(hex_input: &str, bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    fn hexa_digit(x: u8) -> Option<u8> {\n        match x {\n            b'0'..=b'9' => Some(x - b'0'),\n            b'A'..=b'F' => Some(x - b'A' + 10),\n            b'a'..=b'f' => Some(x - b'a' + 10),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n    let inp = hex_input.as_bytes();\n    let input_length = inp.len();\n    if input_length == 0 || (input_length & 1) != 0 || 2 * bin_output.len() < input_length {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    let mut ret = 0;\n    let mut offset = 0;\n    while offset < input_length {\n        match (hexa_digit(inp[offset]), hexa_digit(inp[offset + 1])) {\n            (Some(av), Some(bv)) => {\n                bin_output[ret] = (av << 4) | bv;\n                ret += 1;\n            }\n            _ => return 0,\n        }\n        offset += 2;\n    }\n    ret\n}\n\n/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n/// `Result` return.\npub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    let len = parse_hexa(hex_input, &mut id[..18]);\n    Ok(ConnectionId {\n        id,\n        id_len: len as u8,\n    })\n}\n\n/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\npub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n    w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    for b in connection_id.as_bytes() {\n        write!(w, \"{:02x}\", b).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)?;\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\npub fn compare_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    let ip_cmp = compare_ip_addr(expected, actual);\n    if ip_cmp == Ordering::Equal {\n        expected.port().cmp(&actual.port())\n    } else {\n        ip_cmp\n    }\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n/// struct sockaddr*)`.\npub fn compare_ip_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    match (expected, actual) {\n        (SocketAddr::V4(ex), SocketAddr::V4(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n        (SocketAddr::V6(ex), SocketAddr::V6(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n        // AF_INET (2) < AF_INET6 (10 on Linux), so V4 < V6.\n        (SocketAddr::V4(_), SocketAddr::V6(_)) => Ordering::Less,\n        (SocketAddr::V6(_), SocketAddr::V4(_)) => Ordering::Greater,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\npub fn get_addr_port(addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n    addr.port()\n}\n\n/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\npub fn set_addr_port(addr: &mut SocketAddr, port: u16) {\n    addr.set_port(port);\n}\n\n/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n/// `usize`.\npub fn addr_length(addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(_) => 16, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)\n        SocketAddr::V6(_) => 28, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in6)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\npub fn store_addr(addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n    addr.copied()\n}\n\n/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\npub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    // `SocketAddr` exposes IP octets only by value (`IpAddr::octets()`),\n    // not by reference; safe Rust cannot produce the required borrow.\n    // Callers should use `addr.ip()` with `Ipv4Addr::octets()` /\n    // `Ipv6Addr::octets()` directly.\n    None\n}\n\n/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\npub fn store_text_addr(ip_address_text: &str, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    let ip: IpAddr = ip_address_text\n        .parse()\n        .map_err(|_| Error::InvalidArgument)?;\n    Ok(SocketAddr::new(ip, port))\n}\n\n/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\npub fn addr_text(addr: &SocketAddr, w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(a) => write!(w, \"{}:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n        SocketAddr::V6(a) => write!(w, \"[{}]:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n///\n/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\npub fn store_loopback_addr(addr_family: i32, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    // AF_INET=2 is universal; AF_INET6=10 on Linux, 30 on macOS.\n    const AF_INET: i32 = 2;\n    #[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\n    const AF_INET6: i32 = 30;\n    #[cfg(not(target_os = \"macos\"))]\n    const AF_INET6: i32 = 10;\n\n    if addr_family == AF_INET {\n        store_text_addr(\"127.0.0.1\", port)\n    } else if addr_family == AF_INET6 {\n        store_text_addr(\"::1\", port)\n    } else {\n        Err(Error::InvalidArgument)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n/// the C behaviour.\npub fn set_preferred_address(\n    preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n    v4_text: Option<&str>,\n    v6_text: Option<&str>,\n    preferred_port: u16,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    *preferred = PreferredAddress::default();\n    if let Some(v4) = v4_text {\n        let addr = store_text_addr(v4, preferred_port)?;\n        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V4(_)) {\n            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n        }\n        preferred.v4 = Some(addr);\n    }\n    if let Some(v6) = v6_text {\n        let addr = store_text_addr(v6, preferred_port)?;\n        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V6(_)) {\n            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n        }\n        preferred.v6 = Some(addr);\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n\n/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\npub fn set_solution_dir(solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n    let mut lock = SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap();\n    *lock = solution_dir.map(|s| -> &'static str { Box::leak(s.to_owned().into_boxed_str()) });\n}\n\n/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\npub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n    *SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap()\n}\n\n/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\npub fn get_input_path(\n    target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    solution_path: Option<&str>,\n    file_name: &str,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    let solution_path = solution_path.unwrap_or(DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR);\n    let sep = if solution_path.ends_with(FILE_SEPARATOR) {\n        \"\"\n    } else {\n        FILE_SEPARATOR\n    };\n    write!(target_file_path, \"{}{}{}\", solution_path, sep, file_name).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Portable file open / close.\n//\n// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n//\n// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n// feature.\n\n/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\npub fn file_delete(file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n    std::fs::remove_file(file_name).map_err(|e| e.raw_os_error().unwrap_or(-1))\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n//\n// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n\n/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\npub fn frames_fixed_skip(bytes: &[u8], size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let size = usize::try_from(size).ok()?;\n    bytes.get(size..)\n}\n\n/// Skip a varint-encoded field; returns the suffix.  C:\n/// `frames_varint_skip`.\npub fn frames_varint_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let v_len = varint_len(*bytes.first()?);\n    frames_fixed_skip(bytes, v_len as u64)\n}\n\n/// Decode a varint and return the suffix plus the value.  C:\n/// `frames_varint_decode` with the `*n64` out-parameter\n/// folded into the tuple return.\npub fn frames_varint_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n    let first = *bytes.first()?;\n    let length = varint_len(first);\n    if bytes.len() < length {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut v = (first & 0x3F) as u64;\n    for &b in &bytes[1..length] {\n        v <<= 8;\n        v += b as u64;\n    }\n    Some((&bytes[length..], v))\n}\n\n/// Decode a length-prefixed field's length component as a\n/// `usize`.  C: `frames_varlen_decode`.\npub fn frames_varlen_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], usize)> {\n    let (rest, len) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n    let n = usize::try_from(len).ok()?;\n    Some((rest, n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint8_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u8)> {\n    let (&n, rest) = bytes.split_first()?;\n    Some((rest, n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint16_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u16)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u16::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1]]);\n    Some((&bytes[2..], n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint32_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u32)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u32::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]]);\n    Some((&bytes[4..], n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint64_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u64::from_be_bytes(bytes[..8].try_into().unwrap());\n    Some((&bytes[8..], n))\n}\n\n/// Skip a length-prefixed data field.  C:\n/// `frames_length_data_skip`.\npub fn frames_length_data_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let (rest, length) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n    frames_fixed_skip(rest, length)\n}\n\n/// Decode a connection id and return the suffix.  C:\n/// `frames_cid_decode`.  The output parameter folds into\n/// the tuple return.\npub fn frames_cid_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], ConnectionId)> {\n    let (rest, id_len) = frames_uint8_decode(bytes)?;\n    if id_len as usize > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE || rest.len() < id_len as usize {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    id[..id_len as usize].copy_from_slice(&rest[..id_len as usize]);\n    Some((&rest[id_len as usize..], ConnectionId { id, id_len }))\n}\n\n/// Length of the varint encoding of `n64`.  C:\n/// `size_t frames_varint_encode_length(uint64_t n64)`.\npub fn frames_varint_encode_length(n64: u64) -> usize {\n    if n64 < 64 {\n        1\n    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n        2\n    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n        4\n    } else {\n        8\n    }\n}\n\n/// Decode the wire length of a varint from its first byte.  C\n/// macro: `VARINT_LEN(bytes) (((uint8_t)1) << ((bytes[0] >> 6) & 3))`.\n/// The macro takes the whole buffer; the function takes just the\n/// first byte (the rest is unused in the C expansion).\npub const fn varint_len(byte0: u8) -> usize {\n    1usize << ((byte0 >> 6) & 3)\n}\n\n/// Encode a varint into `bytes`; returns the unused tail or `None`\n/// if the buffer was too small.  C: `frames_varint_encode`.\npub fn frames_varint_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if n64 < 64 {\n        if bytes.is_empty() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n        if bytes.len() < 2 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 8) | 0x40) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n        if bytes.len() < 4 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 24) | 0x80) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n        bytes[3] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n    } else {\n        if bytes.len() < 8 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 56) | 0xC0) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n        bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n        bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n        bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n        bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n        bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n    }\n}\n\n/// Encode a `usize` as a varint length prefix.  C:\n/// `frames_varlen_encode`.\npub fn frames_varlen_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: usize) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    frames_varint_encode(bytes, n as u64)\n}\n\n/// Encode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint8_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u8) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = n;\n    Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint16_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u16) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a 24-bit value (low three bytes of `n`) in network\n/// order.  C: `frames_uint24_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint24_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 3 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[3..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint32_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint64_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 56) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 48) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n >> 40) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = (n >> 32) as u8;\n    bytes[4] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[5] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[6] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[7] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a length prefix followed by the data bytes.  C:\n/// `frames_length_data_encode`.  The C signature took\n/// `(size_t l, const uint8_t* v)` — collapsed to `&[u8]` (slice\n/// length subsumes `l`).\npub fn frames_length_data_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], v: &[u8]) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    let l = v.len();\n    let rest = frames_varlen_encode(bytes, l)?;\n    if rest.len() < l {\n        return None;\n    }\n    rest[..l].copy_from_slice(v);\n    Some(&mut rest[l..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a connection id as length-prefixed data.  C:\n/// `frames_cid_encode`.\npub fn frames_cid_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], cid: &ConnectionId) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, cid.as_bytes())\n}\n\n/// Encode a NUL-terminated C string `s` as length-prefixed data\n/// (the NUL is *not* written).  C: `frames_charz_encode`.\npub fn frames_charz_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], s: &str) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, s.as_bytes())\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Fixed-width big-endian frame-field writers (intformat.c).\n//\n// Write exactly N bytes of a big-endian integer into the start of\n// `bytes`, panicking if the slice is too short (same as the C\n// contract: passing an undersized buffer is undefined behaviour).\n\n/// Write `n16` as a big-endian 16-bit value into `bytes[0..2]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:27-31`.\npub fn picoformat_16(bytes: &mut [u8], n16: u16) {\n    bytes[0] = (n16 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = n16 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write the low 24 bits of `n24` as a big-endian 3-byte value into `bytes[0..3]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:33-38`.\npub fn picoformat_24(bytes: &mut [u8], n24: u32) {\n    bytes[0] = (n24 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n24 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = n24 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write `n32` as a big-endian 32-bit value into `bytes[0..4]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:40-46`.\npub fn picoformat_32(bytes: &mut [u8], n32: u32) {\n    bytes[0] = (n32 >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n32 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n32 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = n32 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write `n64` as a big-endian 64-bit value into `bytes[0..8]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:48-58`.\npub fn picoformat_64(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) {\n    bytes[0] = (n64 >> 56) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n    bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Constant-time memcmp (used for reset secrets).\n\n/// Constant-time three-way compare of two byte buffers of equal\n/// length.  C: `int constant_time_memcmp(const uint8_t*\n/// x, const uint8_t* y, size_t l)` — returning negative / zero /\n/// positive, mapped to [`Ordering`].  The two slice lengths are\n/// the C `l` argument and must match.\npub fn constant_time_memcmp(x: &[u8], y: &[u8]) -> Ordering {\n    let mut acc: u64 = 0;\n    for (&xi, &yi) in x.iter().zip(y.iter()) {\n        acc += (xi ^ yi) as u64;\n    }\n    if acc == 0 {\n        Ordering::Equal\n    } else {\n        Ordering::Less\n    }\n}\n\n// The C `picoquic_thread_t` / `picoquic_mutex_t` / `picoquic_event_t`\n// portability wrappers around pthread / Win32 are gone.  Rust call\n// sites use the standard library directly: `std::thread::JoinHandle`\n// for a thread handle, `std::sync::Mutex<T>` for a lock paired with\n// the value it guards, and `(Mutex<bool>, Condvar)` (or a higher-level\n// channel from `std::sync::mpsc`) where C used `picoquic_event_t`.\n//\n// Likewise the `picoquic_thread_fn` typedef has no Rust counterpart —\n// `std::thread::spawn` already takes an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send`\n// closure, which subsumes the C \"function pointer + void* arg\"\n// pattern.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Random-number helpers.\n\n// `uniform_random` is gone -- the C body read `/dev/urandom`\n// at every call.  Rust callers use `rand::Rng::random_range`\n// on whatever RNG they hold (`Quic.rng` for the secure stream,\n// `rand::rng()` for the non-secure thread-local).\n\n// `test_random` and the `test_*_random` family moved to\n// `crate::tests::util` — they only support the test suite.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Byte-array → text conversion (logging helper).\n\n/// Translate a byte buffer to a printable text form.  C:\n/// `char* uint8_to_str(char* text, size_t text_len, const\n/// uint8_t* data, size_t data_len)` — both length parameters fold\n/// into the slice lengths.  The returned slice is the populated\n/// prefix of `text`.\npub fn uint8_to_str<'a>(text: &'a mut [u8], data: &[u8]) -> &'a [u8] {\n    let text_len = text.len();\n    let data_len = data.len();\n    let render_length = if data_len >= text_len {\n        text_len.saturating_sub(4)\n    } else {\n        data_len\n    };\n    let mut rendered = 0usize;\n    for &c in &data[..render_length] {\n        text[rendered] = if (b' '..127).contains(&c) { c } else { b'?' };\n        rendered += 1;\n    }\n    if rendered < data_len {\n        let mut i = 0usize