{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019DsVyxnSWhtgmU59W1vYTf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the Rust codebase. Let me check how `BBRStartRound` would work in the Rust code. It needs:\n1. `round_start_pn` = `picoquic_cc_get_sequence_number(path_x->cnx, path_x)` which in Rust would be:\n   - If `is_multipath_enabled`: `path_x.pkt_ctx.send_sequence`\n   - Else: `connection.pkt_ctx[PacketContext::Application as usize].send_sequence`\n\n\n2. `next_round_delivered` = `path_x.delivered`\n\nLooking at the existing bbr.rs, I notice there's no `start_round` function yet, and `BBRStartRound` is called within `BBRStartProbeBW_DOWN`, so I'll need to implement it as part of `BBRAdaptUpperBounds`. For that function, I need to handle `CheckInflightTooHigh`, which requires implementing `BBRHandleInflightTooHigh` alongside the existing `is_inflight_too_high` check.\n\nThe dependency chain gets deeper from there—`BBRHandleInflightTooHigh` needs `BBRStartProbeBW_DOWN`, which in turn needs `pick_probe_wait` and `start_round`. All of these are methods on `BbrState`, but there's a structural problem: `BBRStartRound` needs a `Connection` reference to call `picoquic_cc_get_sequence_number`, yet `BBRAdaptUpperBounds` doesn't receive a `Connection` parameter in its C signature.\n\nI could work around this by using `path_x.pkt_ctx.send_sequence` in the Rust version, though that only works reliably in the multipath case. For single-path scenarios, I'd need access to the connection's application context, which suggests the C implementation might have a similar limitation or workaround I'm not seeing yet.\n\nThe real issue is that several BBR functions transitively depend on the connection context, but the current Rust API only passes `path_x` to methods like `enter_startup` and `enter_probe_rtt`. I need to figure out whether to thread the `Connection` parameter through all these methods or find another way to access the sequence number without it. I'm checking whether `BBRPickProbeWait` and `BBRPickProbeWaitEarly` need additional parameters beyond what's already on `BbrState` — they use `random_context` and `min_rtt` which are available directly, but I need to verify how the random number generation works in the Rust version since the C code calls `picoquic_test_uniform_random`.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2178,"cache_read_input_tokens":84202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2178},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da743372-a94e-4c3a-8252-a357471bf140","uuid":"2c37dd3b-4780-421d-bc8c-0c407587faa3"}
